some Democratic lawmakers in California  want to make it the first state to  significantly restrict when police  officers can open fire the proposed  legislation is in direct response to  last month's deadly police shooting of  Stephan Clark he was not armed at the  time Carter Evans is at the California  State Capitol with more on this story  Carter good morning to you good morning  lawmakers want to raise the standard for  when police can use deadly force from  when it's considered too reasonable to  only when it is absolutely necessary to  preserve human life they believe that'll  encourage officers to defuse more  situations but others believe it could  put police lives in danger of grief  tensions remain high in Sacramento more  than two weeks after the police shooting  of 22 year old Stephan Clark at a City  Council meeting Tuesday community  members demanded answers and action you  better give us justice cuz you're damn  sure not ready for what comes next those  cries for justice and reform have now  spread from Sacramento City Hall to the  State Capitol the time is now to act  time for California to step up  California lawmakers plan to introduce  new legislations raising the standard  for when police officers can use deadly  force in the six seconds between the  time officers called out to Stephan  Clark and opened fire current California  law required them to determine whether  the use of deadly force was quote  reasonable but the new law would require  them to determine whether it's not just  reasonable but necessary this policy  authorizes police office to use deadly  force only when it is necessary to  prevent eminent and serious bodily  injury or death this is a split-second  decision in many cases it's not only a  split-second decision but it's a  critical one that'll last forever  Assemblymember and former California  Highway Patrol officer Tom Lackey says  changing the deadly 4 standard could put  an unreasonable burden on officers  caught in dangerous situations is your  concern that it would cause more  questioning at a critical time  when seconds count well clearly that's  going to be the result is that it would  cause maybe the loss of life maybe the  loss of officer life because there would  be hesitation less than a second on many  occasions Assemblyman Lackey says the  better solution here is to improve  police training this bill by the way is  likely to face stiff opposition from law  enforcement organizations their voices  carry a lot of weight here in Sacramento  Fionna they sure do Carter thank you
 

